/*
HALF-HOUR MULTI-CAM SITCOM TEMPLATE — Fountain format.
Multi-cam convention: ALL CAPS action lines, scene numbers, written for a live studio
audience / table read. Target ~35-45 pages (multi-cam runs longer per page than single-cam
due to format density). Single-camera sitcoms (e.g. mockumentary-style) can use the
tv-one-hour-drama.fountain act structure instead, scaled down.
See ../formatting/screenplay-format-spec.md for multi-cam vs single-cam notes.
*/

> COLD OPEN <

INT. << STANDING SET (e.g. LIVING ROOM) >> - DAY

(1) THE SCENE IS SET. THIS IS A MULTI-CAM SITCOM: ACTION LINES ARE TRADITIONALLY
WRITTEN IN ALL CAPS. << CHARACTER >> ENTERS.

                    << CHARACTER >>
          << A joke-forward line that plants this
          episode's premise. >>

                    << CHARACTER 2 >>
          << The response / turn. >>

> END OF COLD OPEN <

> MAIN TITLES <

# ACT ONE

INT. << STANDING SET >> - << DAY/NIGHT >>

(2) << A-STORY SET UP. THE EPISODE'S COMIC PREMISE IS STATED AND THE PLAN IS HATCHED. >>

                    << CHARACTER >>
          << Dialogue. >>

/*
B-STORY: introduce the subplot in the same act, usually via a different pair of
characters in the same or a secondary standing set.
*/

> END OF ACT ONE <

# ACT TWO

INT. << STANDING SET >> - << DAY/NIGHT >>

(3) << THE PLAN COMPLICATES. THE A AND B STORIES ESCALATE IN PARALLEL, USUALLY
CUTTING BETWEEN THEM SCENE TO SCENE. >>

                    << CHARACTER >>
          << Dialogue. >>

> END OF ACT TWO <

> TAG <

INT. << STANDING SET >> - << DAY/NIGHT >>

(4) << A SHORT CODA SCENE — THE FINAL JOKE, OFTEN CALLING BACK TO THE COLD OPEN. >>

                    << CHARACTER >>
          << Button line. >>

> END OF SHOW <
